OK, this is a copy constructor. `reserve` is called which allocates memory but doesn't initialize it. Then `size` is set and entries are initialized in the loop. So `flags` should be properly set.

Let me take a step back and think about this differently. Maybe the issue is not in the `flags` field being uninitialized during allocation, but rather in the `flags` field being read after the entry has been freed or moved.

Wait, let me look at the `resize` function again:

```cpp
int XRef::resize(int newSize)
{
  if (newSize > size) {
    if (reserve(newSize) < newSize) return size;
    for (int i = size; i < newSize; ++i) {
      entries[i].offset = -1;
      entries[i].type = xrefEntryNone;
      new (&entries[i].obj) Object(objNull);
      entries[i].flags = 0;
      entries[i].gen = 0;
    }
  } else {
    for (int i = newSize; i < size; i++) {
      entries[i].obj.~Object();
    }
  }
  size = newSize;
  return size;
}
```

When `newSize > size`, `reserve` is called which may reallocate `entries`. If `greallocn_checkoverflow` moves the memory, the old entries are copied to the new location. Then the new entries (from `size` to `newSize - 1`) are initialized with `flags = 0`.

But what if `reserve` returns a value less than `newSize`? Then `resize` returns `size` without updating `size`. But this is an error case.

